        "text": "Congress has traditionally been expected to authorize military actions, making the proposed exception significant.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "The urgency of the situation justified bypassing normal legislative processes.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Military action based on suspicion rather than confirmed evidence was considered acceptable under emergency conditions.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The term 'War on Terror' would become widely recognized and used to describe U.S. foreign policy following 9/11.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The president holds executive power sufficient to initiate major military operations unilaterally when deemed necessary.",
