    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 76905,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76905.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "You know, the public, quite frankly, are really very emotional about, but they elected us to try to sort all this out for them and to be a member of Congress who can lead. And in that moment, I said, no, this is so important, so serious. I cannot just go with the flow. I've got to clarify what I think is taking place as it relates to this resolution and why this could lead us to a war without end. There was a memorial service. I believe it was that, well, it was on the 14th, I believe. You know, members of Congress attended and I was not going to go because I wanted to stay on the Hill and talk it through with my staff what my vote should be on this resolution and talk to a few more people. But at the last minute I was in what we call the cloak room that's in back of the chamber and talking to one of my colleagues and I said, you know, I think I'm going to go to the memorial service. I probably was the last one on the bus. I raced down the steps.",
