      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Taking a deep breath suggests emotional weight or personal significance attached to the decision.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Barbara Lee’s actions reflect a principled or morally charged stance on the matter.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The presence of all 435 members indicates high legislative engagement or importance of the issue.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The narrative framing emphasizes individual agency within institutional processes.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Congressional votes typically occur following ceremonial or symbolic events like religious services.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Topic Shift"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 76905,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/6/76905.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "I rise today really with a very heavy heart. one that is filled with sorrow for the families and the loved ones who were killed and injured this week. Only the most foolish and the most counts would not understand the grief that has really gripped our people and millions across the world. This unspeakable act on the United States has really forced me, however, to rely on my moral compass, my conscience, and my God for direction. September 11th changed the world. Our deepest fear is now haunt us. Yet I am convinced that military action will not prevent further acts of international terrorism against the United States. It was very emotional, but also looking out, I knew a lot of them felt the same way I felt because a lot of them said that to me. And I didn't sense anger. I sensed more. Like, well, that's her point of view. She really doesn't get it, you know, or she's clueless. And that I was looking at the world through rose colored glasses, which was not the case at all. Our country is in a state of mourning. Some of us must say let's step back for a moment. Let's just pause just for a minute and think through the implications of our actions today so that this does not spiral out of control. Congressman Henry Hyde, who was managing the bill and the late Tom Lantos, the Democrat, was also on the floor as well as our leader, Steny Hoyer. And I remember after I spoke, I walked forward to go sit back down. And I think all three of them came up and hugged me because they knew what I felt and how I felt. And they all three told me why they disagreed with me. They were very proud of me and they understood that this was my conscience as well as voting what I thought was the correct vote.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_05",
